  "body": "Tormach microARC 4 as an A rotary about X: a new 770MX + microARC 4 machine file, and indexed 4-axis work in the PathPilot post. Simultaneous 4-axis is refused by name and split off to #796. Includes the hardware test kit the user asked for.\n\nFork synced at v0.16.0.\n\n## What changed\n- **Machine file** `assets/machines/tormach-770mx-microarc4.mch` (\"Tormach 770MX + microARC 4\", registered in `posting::MACHINES`): the 770MX's figures plus an A rotary part, `axis: [1, 0, 0]`, travel -360..360 degrees. No rates are stated; TD10749 gives none, and absent is never zero.\n  - The **direction sense** is the sign of the axis vector: `[1,0,0]` is RS-274 / ISO 841 (a + A turns the tool right-handed about +X relative to the part), and `[-1,0,0]` negates the posted A. This is documented in `assets/machines/AGENTS.md`.\n- **Post** `assets/posts/tormach.cps`:\n  - A tilted section is indexed at its first motion: `G53 G0 Z<safe>`, then `G0 A..` alone, then the X Y first move.\n  - The section's tips are posted in the turned frame: relative to the work offset, turned by -A about X. So **WCS Y0 Z0 must lie on the A centerline**, and the post writes an operator note at the first index.\n  - A is read **through the tool axis**, not the letters, so the pose law's canonical A0 B180 (the face underneath) posts as `G0 A180`.\n  - A machine with an A writes `G0 A..` at its first motion, `A0` for upright work: a program never assumes where the last run left the table. A machine without one writes no A at all, so every existing golden is byte-identical.\n  - No A word ever rides a cutting move, and no clamp code is written.\n- **Refused by name:** a tilt the A axis can't reach (any X component in the tool axis, any C); an A past the stated travel; an A that changes inside an operation (\"simultaneous 4-axis motion ... not confirmed on PathPilot\"); an arc on an indexed A; and a tilt on a machine with no A about X.\n- **Parser fix found on the way** (`crates/post/src/machine/parse.rs`): the head search used `get(\"parts\")?`, so it stopped at the first leaf part. Any file with a leaf (like the new A rotary) ahead of its head silently lost its spindle range, belt ranges, rigid tapping and coolant. It now passes leaves over.\n- **Test kit** `docs/tester-kits/tormach-microarc-4th/`:\n  - The part: 3/4\" bar, 1.5\" stick-out, G54 X0 at the end face, Y0 Z0 on the A centerline, A0 at a marked line.\n  - The cuts: two opposite 0.015\" flats at A-90 / A90 (1/4\" end mill), and a 0.020\" cross groove back at A0 (1/8\" end mill).\n  - The files: the project built by the app (`microarc_test_part.camproj`), its posted program (`microarc_test_part.nc`, pinned), and a README with the setup, the safety steps (A-direction check by MDI `G0 A90`, collet-nut clearance, air-cut with Z raised and single-block) and a checklist.\n  - The continuous op (a Rotary Parallel wrap on the same bar) is refused by name now and is pinned as #796's test.\n- Decisions: `docs/decisions/785-microarc-4th-axis.md`.\n\n## The three questions (answered on #785)\n1. **Wrapped or linear A?** No source settles it. The Tormach guides are silent, and LinuxCNC leaves rollover to each machine's configuration. So: indexing only, and any A that changes inside an operation is refused.\n2. **Clamp or brake M-code?** None. The microARC 4 (harmonic drive) has no clamp, and the older tables (TD10552, TD10374) clamp by hand and must be released to rotate. The post writes an operator note instead.\n3. **Combined XYZA feed?** Under LinuxCNC G94, F is the XYZ feed and A is carried along; G93 is listed in Tormach's guidelines. Neither is confirmed on the microARC, so nothing combined is posted: A only ever moves alone, with Z up. Split off to #796.\n\n## How tested\n- **`crates/app/tests/tormach_fourth_axis.rs`** (new, 10 tests):\n  - `the_microarc_machine_states_an_a_about_x`: the machine schema, plus the parser fix.\n  - `an_indexed_four_face_job_posts_a_between_operations`: the golden `golden/nc/tormach/index_4face_microarc4.nc`. Each face's posted square equals the hand-rotated local square.\n  - `an_upright_job_states_a0_on_the_4th_axis_machine_only`, `a_reversed_a_vector_posts_the_angle_negated`, `the_canonical_b180_pose_posts_as_a180`.\n  - The refusals: `a_tilt_the_a_axis_cannot_reach_is_refused`, `an_a_past_travel_is_refused`, `an_a_that_changes_inside_an_operation_is_refused`, `an_arc_on_an_indexed_a_is_refused`, `a_tilt_on_a_machine_with_no_a_about_x_is_refused`.\n- **`crates/app/tests/microarc_test_part.rs`** (new):\n  - `the_microarc_test_part_posts_its_indexed_program`: the kit is built through the app's commands, passes the verification gate, posts byte-equal to the kit `.nc`, and its project file names its ops.\n  - `a_wrapped_op_on_the_test_part_is_refused_by_name`: the gate refuses the wrap op, and the post refuses it as simultaneous 4-axis.\n- **Each refusal fails its test when removed.** Five mutants of `tormach.cps`, each with one refusal disabled; each fails exactly its pinning test (9 pass, 1 fail per mutant).\n- Ran green locally: conformance (no other post's golden changed), machine, machine_library, machine_edit, tormach_post, tormach_machines, tormach_safe_retract, probe_tormach, tilt, rotary_letter, posting, camcraft-post unit tests, and `cargo clippy -p camcraft-post -p camcraft-app --all-targets -D warnings`.\n- Linux quick gate (`--base upstream/main`): green, post suites + app suites, 9.2 min, 0 failed.\n\n## Not exercised\n- The PathPilot Hub, and the user's 770MX with a microARC 4. Those runs come later; the kit is ready for them.\n- The real direction sense of the microARC 4 on PathPilot: the kit's first step checks it.\n- **Known limit (#797):** the app's machine check judges tilt letters with no IK, so A0 B180 (the face at 180 degrees) is refused at the program gate even though the post can write it. The kit uses A-90 / A90 for the opposite pair.\n- Other mills with a microARC 4 (1100MX and so on): copy the file and add the rotary part.\n\nChangelog: Tormach PathPilot post indexes a 4th axis (A about X) between operations, with the new Tormach 770MX + microARC 4 machine; simultaneous 4-axis, unreachable tilts and A past travel are refused by name; machine files with a leaf part ahead of the head keep their spindle and coolant figures\n\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nRefs #785\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01CUsqDAAAJyAPrC37zBzbbX\n",
